WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.210
Who could have predicted
that one of the Internet's

00:00:02.293 --> 00:00:03.962
most elaborate
fandom communities

00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:05.672
would begin with a single post

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:07.674
on June 22nd, 2007?

00:00:07.757 --> 00:00:10.635
Uploaded on 4chan's
Paranormal X board,

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:13.012
the statute read like
a government case file,

00:00:13.096 --> 00:00:14.806
and opened with
a provocative warning:

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:16.975
"Special containment procedures,

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:20.145
"Item SCP-173 is to be
kept in a locked container

00:00:20.228 --> 00:00:21.730
at all times."

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:24.149
The entry describes an
animate and malevolent figure,

00:00:24.232 --> 00:00:25.775
made of concrete and rebar,

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:28.987
with indications it had been
spray-painted at one time.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.614
It is housed in a large room
with unbarred windows.

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:33.533
When alone,
sounds of stone scraping

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:34.701
can be heard.

00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:35.952
It will fill its container

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:38.163
with a mixture of
reddish brown material,

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:41.040
believed to be a combination
of feces and blood,

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:42.500
but the scariest part,

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:44.169
blink and it will kill you.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:46.546
Although the post would be
deleted a few days later,

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:49.048
the strange entry
sparked the imagination

00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:50.467
of the X community,

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.801
suggesting the existence

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.678
of a secret government
organization

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:54.888
dedicated to collecting

00:00:54.971 --> 00:00:57.140
and containing
preternatural entities.

00:00:57.223 --> 00:01:00.268
Yeah, that's the perfect
start for worldbuilding.

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:01.770
[spirited music]

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:03.480
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.940
and this is "Monstrum."

00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:08.193
This strange entry in 2007

00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:10.069
was the genesis of
what we now know

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:11.905
as the SCP Foundation,

00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:13.948
a spooky, fictional universe

00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:16.826
centered around a Wiki that
hosts thousands of posts,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.953
from contributors
across the globe

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:20.538
who contribute to its lore.

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:23.249
SCP Foundation is
crowdsourced folklore

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:24.709
at some of its finest.

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.461
And it takes
collaborative fiction

00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:28.254
to a whole new dimension.

00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:30.340
While it's tempting
to dismiss the SCP

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.425
as some crazy
conspiracy website,

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:35.220
the administrators clearly
and frequently acknowledge

00:01:35.303 --> 00:01:37.430
that the Foundation
is essentially an art

00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:39.015
and creative writing experiment.

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:41.017
In their words, "The Foundation,

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.894
"its anomalies,
and everything else

00:01:42.977 --> 00:01:44.854
that's been written
about is fictional."

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:47.190
But that doesn't
make it any less fun.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.399
Quite the opposite.

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:49.400
According to the lore,

00:01:49.484 --> 00:01:50.944
the SCP Foundation

00:01:51.027 --> 00:01:53.738
is a nebulous international
group of individuals,

00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:56.658
who identify, capture,
and contain supernatural

00:01:56.741 --> 00:01:58.827
and preternatural anomalies,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.996
with the full support of every
major national government.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.249
Kind of like the
"Men in Black" and "Area 51"

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:05.667
meets "The X-Files."

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:06.960
Their official slogan--

00:02:07.043 --> 00:02:09.546
"We secure, we contain,
we protect."

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.006
Objects like the
malevolent IKEA store,

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.508
Shy Guy, The Old Man,

00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:15.385
or the endless staircase,

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:17.470
are iconic
to the Foundation's lore.

00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:19.681
If you've been on any horror
corner of the internet,

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:21.599
you've probably felt
the lingering presence

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:23.601
of the SCP in games,

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:25.854
short films,
fiction, and fan art.

00:02:25.937 --> 00:02:28.064
Part of that is because
instead of being beholden

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:29.816
to copyright and ownership,

00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:32.360
which can restrict
a story's ability to adapt,

00:02:32.443 --> 00:02:36.030
each SCP universe entry is
part of a collective tradition

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:37.490
that is ever-evolving.

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:39.242
And the lore spreads quickly.

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.994
While many people,

00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:42.287
rightfully, tend to hold
older legends and folklore

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.290
in reverence for their
historical significance

00:02:45.373 --> 00:02:46.791
and insights into
evolving communities,

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:50.461
the SCP Foundation
offers something more,

00:02:50.545 --> 00:02:53.173
a unique perspective into
how stories and monsters

00:02:53.256 --> 00:02:55.049
evolve in real time.

00:02:55.133 --> 00:02:56.759
The community is
open to any member

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:58.052
who would like to post.

00:02:58.136 --> 00:03:00.638
Most entries are told
through case files,

00:03:00.722 --> 00:03:03.600
fiction, written in
a pseudoscientific report style,

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.977
that details the special
containment procedures

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.562
of the anomaly,

00:03:07.645 --> 00:03:09.230
followed by a detailed
description of the event,

00:03:09.314 --> 00:03:10.481
object, or entity,

00:03:10.565 --> 00:03:11.900
and how it was contained.

00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:13.234
Each of these entries

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:15.278
is given a three- or
four-digit SCP number

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.321
and brief title.

00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:17.697
Some images may be included,

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.073
as well as addendums

00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:20.491
that contribute to
the understanding

00:03:20.575 --> 00:03:21.659
of the artifact,

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:23.369
and how it is
affecting or responding

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:24.871
to its surroundings.

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:26.539
In more recent years,

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:28.583
creative authors have even
produced interactive pages

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:31.794
that evoke all sorts of
real-world technologies.

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:33.796
Unlike many other fictional
supernatural organizations,

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.092
the SCP Foundation is
incredibly bureaucratic,

00:03:38.176 --> 00:03:40.553
which contributes to
a successful worldbuilding.

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:42.513
Bureaucracy is
something many of us

00:03:42.597 --> 00:03:44.599
are all well too familiar with.

00:03:44.682 --> 00:03:47.477
And utilizing this common
cultural understanding

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.395
allows for
a more-ready entry point

00:03:49.479 --> 00:03:51.064
into the Foundation's world.

00:03:51.147 --> 00:03:54.484
Supporting this connection
is SCP's rhetorical style,

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.611
which mimics classified
government texts,

00:03:56.694 --> 00:03:58.780
especially redacted documents.

00:03:58.863 --> 00:04:00.239
Common themes can be found

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:02.367
throughout the
Foundation's many entries.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.661
Fear or reticence of technology,

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.329
viral communication,

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:09.165
scientific or governmental
failure to control danger,

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:10.583
definitions of sentience,

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:13.002
doppelganger and
hybrid-being anxiety,

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:15.213
but that's just
the tip of the iceberg.

00:04:15.296 --> 00:04:17.257
There's Josie, the half cat,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.801
hidden exoplanets
with alien life forms,

00:04:19.884 --> 00:04:21.094
a coffee vending machine

00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:22.929
that can produce any
liquid on demand,

00:04:23.012 --> 00:04:25.264
a strangely helpful,
star-shaped nightlight,

00:04:25.348 --> 00:04:26.891
a pool of what
appears to be blood

00:04:26.975 --> 00:04:28.893
from which extremely
hostile entities

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:30.228
occasionally exit,

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:31.896
and the remains of
a murdered woman

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.565
that cause horrific
deaths and disappearances.

00:04:34.649 --> 00:04:35.608
These objects,

00:04:35.692 --> 00:04:37.610
along with thousands of others,

00:04:37.694 --> 00:04:39.696
are labeled as one
of 11 object classes.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:42.615
Separated into common
primary classes

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:44.826
and non-standard object classes,

00:04:44.909 --> 00:04:46.744
the categories range from safe,

00:04:46.828 --> 00:04:47.704
meaning threatening,

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:49.205
but easily and safely contained

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:50.957
with minimal
Foundational effort,

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.251
to anomalies that
require massive effort

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:54.669
to be dealt with.

00:04:54.752 --> 00:04:56.629
Usually associated with
a world-ending threat.

00:04:56.713 --> 00:04:59.132
Objects in this class
cannot be contained,

00:04:59.215 --> 00:05:00.258
are indestructible,

00:05:00.341 --> 00:05:02.468
and/or are expected to escape.

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:04.262
An object's class is determined

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:07.181
by the informal Locked Box Test.

00:05:07.265 --> 00:05:08.725
According to Foundation lore,

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.561
most autonomous, alive,
and/or sapien objects,

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:13.813
are, at least, Euclid-class,

00:05:13.896 --> 00:05:15.732
meaning, you can
lock it in a box,

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.608
but you're not sure
what will happen.

00:05:17.692 --> 00:05:20.236
The authors of these texts
are tapping into attention

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.655
that exists across
many populations,

00:05:22.739 --> 00:05:23.990
fear that our governments

00:05:24.073 --> 00:05:25.825
may not be telling
us the whole story,

00:05:25.908 --> 00:05:29.245
and raise questions about if
such information withholding

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:31.789
is productive or destructive.

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:33.041
And that's exactly
what the original,

00:05:33.124 --> 00:05:36.002
2007 4chan post provided,

00:05:36.085 --> 00:05:37.587
a story that taps
into people's fears

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.380
and mistrust of the government,

00:05:39.464 --> 00:05:42.550
as well as their ability to
contain their secrets safely.

00:05:42.633 --> 00:05:44.052
We might not have
known the source

00:05:44.135 --> 00:05:46.471
of the original SCP-173

00:05:46.554 --> 00:05:48.097
if it weren't for
the online sleuth

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.391
dasMaymay, who brought
the original entry

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:52.060
back to the Internet's awareness

00:05:52.143 --> 00:05:54.771
after some archive
searching in 2018.

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:56.314
Fans of Dr. Who

00:05:56.397 --> 00:05:58.357
may note the similarities
between the weeping angels

00:05:58.441 --> 00:06:00.234
and SCP-173.

00:06:00.318 --> 00:06:02.445
While this connection
has not been confirmed,

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:03.863
for Dr. Who fans,

00:06:03.946 --> 00:06:05.656
this narrative serves
as an extension

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.701
of something that already
exists in popular culture,

00:06:08.785 --> 00:06:10.870
but brings it
into a larger organization,

00:06:10.953 --> 00:06:13.081
and hints at
its real-world existence

00:06:13.164 --> 00:06:16.042
by using familiar bureaucratic
language and style.

00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:17.877
Administrators of
the Wiki now note

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:21.214
that SCP-173 is
uniquely inspiring,

00:06:21.297 --> 00:06:23.382
because it relies on
the fear that emerges

00:06:23.466 --> 00:06:25.259
from wonder and possibility,

00:06:25.343 --> 00:06:26.844
rather than a jump scare.

00:06:26.928 --> 00:06:29.305
The original five large
threads that emerged

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:32.141
on 4chans X Forum
in January, 2008,

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:34.852
dedicated to expanding
the SCP universe,

00:06:34.936 --> 00:06:37.730
became known as the SCP Series.

00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:40.149
The popularity of the work
was deemed important enough,

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:41.526
or cool enough,

00:06:41.609 --> 00:06:43.653
that, on January 19th,
someone made a Wiki

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:45.863
for all of the SCP-themed posts

00:06:45.947 --> 00:06:47.490
that began popping up,

00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:50.326
so that the content wouldn't
be lost to the ether.

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:52.286
Originally housed on
the EditThis Wiki farm,

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.789
anyone at any time
could edit the stories.

00:06:54.872 --> 00:06:57.500
Discussion boards
existed for every page.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:59.836
User, FritzWillie, saw that
stories were being deleted

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:01.587
by the EditThis hosting network,

00:07:01.671 --> 00:07:04.507
and moved the entirety of
the community's content

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:05.800
to a WikiDot,

00:07:05.883 --> 00:07:08.761
which went live on
July 25th, 2008.

00:07:08.845 --> 00:07:11.556
This move introduced a page
rating and comment system,

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:12.807
and moderators were introduced.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:15.601
Everything was
still open source.

00:07:15.685 --> 00:07:18.312
Any user could edit
any post they wanted.

00:07:18.396 --> 00:07:20.106
Until someone
created spam threads

00:07:20.189 --> 00:07:22.650
and started deleting
stuff en masse.

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:25.444
Longtime users decided to
remove anonymous edits,

00:07:25.528 --> 00:07:27.947
and an administration
team emerged.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:31.159
Eventually, to keep the spirit
of the original SCP Series,

00:07:31.242 --> 00:07:33.870
and ensure that the quality
of content was maintained,

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:35.163
banning was enacted,

00:07:35.246 --> 00:07:37.456
and more strict editing
policies put in place.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:38.749
The community's dedication

00:07:38.833 --> 00:07:39.959
to preserving the
early work of the genre

00:07:40.042 --> 00:07:42.837
is not just something
I appreciate;

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.380
it's absolutely crucial

00:07:44.463 --> 00:07:46.424
to establishing any
kind of standard

00:07:46.507 --> 00:07:48.259
or canon for the genre.

00:07:48.342 --> 00:07:50.595
I also think it shows
a really lovely dedication

00:07:50.678 --> 00:07:52.471
to the Foundation's community.

00:07:52.555 --> 00:07:54.765
The internet can often feel
like a lawless country,

00:07:54.849 --> 00:07:56.017
but when it comes to
the SCP Foundation,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.728
rules help preserve
its integrity.

00:07:58.811 --> 00:08:00.897
Because most of the
content posted to the site

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.148
is allowed to be adapted,

00:08:02.231 --> 00:08:04.066
recreated, or reused,

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.485
The Foundation's universe
has served as inspiration

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:08.863
for many other creative works.

00:08:08.946 --> 00:08:10.656
There was a theater
contribution to the lore,

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:11.908
Welcome to the Ethics Committee,

00:08:11.991 --> 00:08:13.159
performed in Dublin,

00:08:13.242 --> 00:08:14.744
a concert in Glasgow,

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:16.204
various video games,

00:08:16.287 --> 00:08:17.622
a serialized novel in Japan,

00:08:17.705 --> 00:08:19.290
and a graphic novel.

00:08:19.373 --> 00:08:21.667
While these elements testify
to the widespread popularity

00:08:21.751 --> 00:08:24.086
of the SCP Foundation universe,

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:27.215
its primary presence
online seems most important

00:08:27.298 --> 00:08:28.591
to its worldbuilding.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:30.384
The internet is
inherently liminal,

00:08:30.468 --> 00:08:31.552
which can make sharing stories

00:08:31.636 --> 00:08:32.803
that seem real, but aren't,

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:34.096
more efficient.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:35.890
The SCP's case-file approach,

00:08:35.973 --> 00:08:37.725
and vaguely scientific rhetoric,

00:08:37.808 --> 00:08:39.685
blurs the boundaries
between the real

00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:40.811
and the not-real,

00:08:40.895 --> 00:08:42.438
in a space many people approach

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:44.649
as both permanent and nebulous.

00:08:44.732 --> 00:08:46.692
Virtual reality is a ready space

00:08:46.776 --> 00:08:48.986
for imagined realities.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.947
But that's not to say there
aren't educational lessons

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:52.740
in the archives of the SCP,

00:08:52.823 --> 00:08:54.784
or that they don't
contribute to social policing

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:57.078
and understandings
of deviant behavior.

00:08:57.161 --> 00:08:59.497
Like all explorations
into monsters and fear,

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.040
it's about pushing
the boundaries

00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:02.917
of what scares or unsettles us,

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.377
and why.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.170
These elements are even debated

00:09:06.254 --> 00:09:08.381
in the Foundation's
discussion boards.

00:09:08.464 --> 00:09:10.508
Take something like SCP-1919,

00:09:10.591 --> 00:09:12.051
for instance,

00:09:12.134 --> 00:09:14.512
a Euclid-class object that
requires a containment perimeter

00:09:14.595 --> 00:09:16.514
with a two-kilometer radius,

00:09:16.597 --> 00:09:18.099
since it presents as a decaying,

00:09:18.182 --> 00:09:21.477
early 20th-century hotel
and converted mansion.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.396
If a human enters the building,

00:09:23.479 --> 00:09:27.066
it produces multiple humanoid
replicas of that person,

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:29.652
as well as anything
they are carrying.

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:31.028
For this reason,

00:09:31.112 --> 00:09:33.531
personnel cannot carry
body armor or weapons.

00:09:33.614 --> 00:09:35.908
These Replicants are
not perfect bones.

00:09:35.992 --> 00:09:37.159
They have physical
abnormalities,

00:09:37.243 --> 00:09:38.202
like sealed nostrils,

00:09:38.286 --> 00:09:39.662
or improper proportions

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:41.789
that mark them as Replicants.

00:09:41.872 --> 00:09:44.166
There are enough of these
that after Expedition Two,

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:45.418
when aggressive Replicants

00:09:45.501 --> 00:09:49.255
referred to as
SCP-19191 emerged.

00:09:49.338 --> 00:09:50.631
There are apparently
enough of these

00:09:50.715 --> 00:09:53.509
that they function as
a coordinated ant colony,

00:09:53.592 --> 00:09:56.512
made and controlled by
a singular female being.

00:09:56.596 --> 00:09:58.180
Any entry is currently halted

00:09:58.264 --> 00:10:00.933
until all manifestations
can be expected

00:10:01.017 --> 00:10:02.601
to have starved to death.

00:10:02.685 --> 00:10:04.729
Okay, there's a lot
to unpack there,

00:10:04.812 --> 00:10:06.439
but the hive zombie mentality,

00:10:06.522 --> 00:10:07.773
aggressive mob violence,

00:10:07.857 --> 00:10:09.525
and mysterious Replicant leader

00:10:09.608 --> 00:10:11.652
called to mind many
real-world fears

00:10:11.736 --> 00:10:13.112
of overpopulation,

00:10:13.195 --> 00:10:14.196
destructive groupthink,

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.823
and even urban decay,

00:10:15.906 --> 00:10:18.409
giving the buildings
decrepit appearance.

00:10:18.492 --> 00:10:21.162
But those are just
my interpretations.

00:10:21.245 --> 00:10:23.122
I think that's where
a lot of the real genius

00:10:23.205 --> 00:10:25.249
comes from
in the SCP Foundation.

00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:27.001
Just enough
information is given,

00:10:27.084 --> 00:10:28.961
and given impassively enough,

00:10:29.045 --> 00:10:30.504
that your mind goes crazy,

00:10:30.588 --> 00:10:32.423
trying to fill in the blanks.

00:10:32.506 --> 00:10:35.426
There seem to be no completed
stories or case files,

00:10:35.509 --> 00:10:37.261
which means infinite exploration

00:10:37.345 --> 00:10:39.305
of what scares us is possible.

00:10:39.388 --> 00:10:40.681
In my opinion,

00:10:40.765 --> 00:10:42.391
in some ways, there's
even greater potential

00:10:42.475 --> 00:10:44.018
for legend growth,

00:10:44.101 --> 00:10:45.686
and significance through
online global communities,

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:47.563
like the SCP Foundation.

00:10:47.646 --> 00:10:49.190
They allow stories
to cultivate quickly

00:10:49.273 --> 00:10:50.524
and in nuanced ways

00:10:50.608 --> 00:10:52.068
that translate
their relatability

00:10:52.151 --> 00:10:53.319
across disparate communities.

00:10:53.402 --> 00:10:55.905
Telling these stories
is performative,

00:10:55.988 --> 00:10:58.032
a form of communication
that allows for others

00:10:58.115 --> 00:10:59.617
to offer their own alterations

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.494
to the text in real-time.

00:11:01.577 --> 00:11:03.371
The appreciation for recreating

00:11:03.454 --> 00:11:04.580
and retelling the stories,

00:11:04.663 --> 00:11:06.999
without fear of
copyright infringement,

00:11:07.083 --> 00:11:10.252
is an interpretive freedom
that's actually really powerful.

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:13.047
It hearkens back to more
traditional lore evolution,

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:15.883
just without geographic
or language boundaries.

00:11:15.966 --> 00:11:18.135
While, "We secure,
we contain, we protect"

00:11:18.219 --> 00:11:19.470
is their official slogan,

00:11:19.553 --> 00:11:22.056
dare I add, "We teach"?

00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:24.266
While not an educational tool
by design,

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:25.893
the collaborative format

00:11:25.976 --> 00:11:29.188
can advance participants'
multimedia communication skills.

00:11:29.271 --> 00:11:30.189
The creative guidelines,

00:11:30.272 --> 00:11:31.232
open authorship,

00:11:31.315 --> 00:11:33.150
an informal peer review process

00:11:33.234 --> 00:11:35.027
covers many
of the fundamental components

00:11:35.111 --> 00:11:37.279
of traditional
writing practices.

00:11:37.363 --> 00:11:39.198
Invention,
collaboration, drafting,

00:11:39.281 --> 00:11:40.825
revision, and reflection,

00:11:40.908 --> 00:11:43.494
those all happen
in the SCP wiki.

00:11:43.577 --> 00:11:45.037
It embodies storytelling methods

00:11:45.121 --> 00:11:46.622
with digital media literacy.

00:11:46.705 --> 00:11:49.083
Is it a substitute for
a formal writing course?

00:11:49.166 --> 00:11:51.001
ASU professor, Emily Zarka, PhD,

00:11:51.085 --> 00:11:52.670
says no.

00:11:52.753 --> 00:11:55.881
But it can serve as
a supplemental educational tool,

00:11:55.965 --> 00:11:57.925
especially for writers
who may be hesitant

00:11:58.008 --> 00:12:00.261
to jump into creative
writing spaces,

00:12:00.344 --> 00:12:02.346
or for authors looking
for a community.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:05.141
Heck, maybe it has
a place in my syllabus.

00:12:05.224 --> 00:12:06.767
SCP Foundation is building

00:12:06.851 --> 00:12:09.311
and supporting a vibrant
and creative community

00:12:09.395 --> 00:12:11.730
that reformulates the
crucial human custom

00:12:11.814 --> 00:12:13.732
of storytelling
and lore building.

00:12:13.816 --> 00:12:17.653
And it has the added bonus
of being creepy AF.

00:12:17.736 --> 00:12:19.447
An anonymity of reports.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:20.781
Anonymity.

00:12:20.865 --> 00:12:22.283
An anonymity of reports.

00:12:22.366 --> 00:12:23.159
I did it again,

00:12:23.242 --> 00:12:24.994
anonymity of reports.

00:12:25.077 --> 00:12:25.953
[bystander chuckles]

00:12:26.036 --> 00:12:27.538
There's too many Ws in a row.

